  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Missouri received $9.2B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 34.0% year over year, and the state saw 94,683 awards at an average award size of $97.4K. The Department of Defense drove the largest share of spending, obligating $6.2B, while the Department of Energy obligated $2.1B. Aircraft Manufacturing and Facilities Support Services together account for a large portion of industry obligations, with Aircraft Manufacturing at $2.7B and Facilities Support Services at $2.2B. THE BOEING COMPANY and HONEYWELL FEDERAL MANUFACTURING & TECHNOLOGIES, LLC are the highest-dollar vendors, with THE BOEING COMPANY appearing twice in the top list at $3.8B and $671M and HONEYWELL at $2.1B. Year-to-date totals are partial because the fiscal year is ongoing, so FY26 figures are not final. Historical context shows prior-year obligations of $16.2B in FY24 and $14B in FY25, illustrating that FY2026 to date is a lower-volume period compared with several recent fiscal years.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the FY2026 spending pattern in Missouri?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $6.2B, and the Department of Energy obligated $2.1B, concentrating most obligations in a few buyers. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry with $2.7B, and Facilities Support Services follows with $2.2B, reflecting heavy procurement in aerospace and facilities work. Obligations fell 34.0% year over year, so FY2026 to date represents a step down from several prior fiscal years."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers and vendors in Missouri right now?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense, the Department of Energy, and the Department of Agriculture lead buying with obligations of $6.2B, $2.1B, and $208M respectively. THE BOEING COMPANY and HONEYWELL FEDERAL MANUFACTURING & TECHNOLOGIES, LLC are the top vendors with $3.8B and $2.1B in obligations. Aircraft Manufacturing at $2.7B and Facilities Support Services at $2.2B are the top industry recipients."
      }
    ]
